{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: exact head 8793316b4a4affc7e41ada79af521618477878b7 on builder/identifiers-vectors-b-20261110, all 25 artifacts, for request a30f1b67 under promise 5a78cc01 and review request d39e5ee5. The design holds, and the evidence is complete. One defect blocks approval: the Git reader takes a parent from a header line that Git itself does not read as a parent, so the verifier reports `ok` for a citation that Git's own history refutes.\n\nRequired change:\n\n1. Read parents as Git does. `gitEvent` (`packages/replay/src/citation.ts:52`) collects every header line that starts with `parent `, wherever it falls. Git reads parents only from the run of `parent` lines directly after `tree`. It ignores a later `parent` line, and `git fsck` refuses the commit. Section 6 of the protocol note defines the predecessor as \"the first parent\", and section 7 says the verifier reads \"the parent lines of the raw commit\".\n\n   My probe, run in my own extracted copy: I took `git256` event 1 from `vectors/git256/sequence.json` and moved its `parent` line to after `author`. Then I cited the result as position 1 under the published genesis `6fb2a11d…`. `verifyCitation` at level 2 returned `{\"result\":\"ok\",\"reached\":2,\"reason\":null,\"anchor\":\"genesis\"}`. In a scratch SHA-256 repository, `git log --format=%P` prints no parent for the same object, `e1bed0b2…`. To Git, that commit is a root and belongs to no other sequence. The verifier gives it position 1 under someone else's genesis. A position 0 citation of the same bytes fails in the opposite direction.\n\n   Make the parents the `parent` lines that directly follow `tree`. Either read a later `parent` line as Git does, or refuse the commit as `mismatch`/`position`. State the choice in section 7. The producer's `commit` (`scripts/identifier-vectors.py:189`) writes only well-formed headers, so add one producer case for each Git format with the expected report. The tests then read it like every other vector.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Source. Bytes parses a citation by consuming the whole string, with exact digest width and a safe decimal position. Unknown formats keep the generic grammar. `verifyCitation` copies each reply before it hashes, hashes before it decodes, and checks dap1 bytes for canonical form. Depth counts every edge, including the edge to an explicit anchor. Conflicting anchors throw before any read, and fetched bytes never become anchors. A signature failure keeps the anchor and trusts of level 2. The Git source runs fixed commands with no inherited config, replace refs, lazy fetch or credentials. It reads the object size with `cat-file --batch-check` and the raw bytes with `cat-file commit`. Native verify, integrity mode and judgment Replay keep their bodies. `main` dispatches to `citationMain` only on a first argument of `--citation`.\n- Independent vectors. `scripts/identifier-vectors.py` imports only the standard library. I ran it once with `python3 -I`, writing to a new scratch directory. It passed its three RFC 8032 known answers, produced 30, 26 and 26 cases, and reproduced all six committed vector files byte for byte. The tests read those files and never call the producer.\n- Tests, on my own extracted copy, with no shared validation slot. `packages/bytes/test/citation.test.ts` passes 2 of 2. `citation.test.ts`, `citation-git.test.ts` and `cli.test.ts` in Replay pass 13 of 13. `node --test scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs scripts/security-settings.test.mjs` passes 15 of 15, including the new Bytes index seal `7c8c9514…`.\n- Scope. Main ab802614 is an ancestor, the head is pushed, and the builder's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the 25 paths that its 25 artifacts cover. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings. The commit-message check passes: 7 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean, and `check-doc-links.mjs` checks 1,238 local links and finds none broken.\n- Evidence. From 377b2adb to the head, only the note and the descriptor change. From fa6e7419 to 377b2adb, only `apps/room/page/assets.ts` changes, and its SHA-256 is `e104ec88…0530`, as the note states. The 62 files in the descriptor match their SHA-256 values in `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-11-identifiers-b-final-377b/unit`. `SHA256SUMS` verifies, and the aggregate manifest hashes to `f632859d…5076`. The failed gate's summary records gate exit 1 and 1,130 Native obligations unreached. The continuation's summary records every exit 0, 1,146 current cases, 1,144 passes and two planned skips. GitHub reports Page run 43078195600 and Card run 43078195494 as attempt 1, completed and successful, at the head. Ruling 89d505ad accepts the late cheap checks.\n\nArchitecture: sound, apart from the required change. Citation integrity stays out of the kernel's Native judgment. Parsing belongs in Bytes, and verification and byte sources belong in Replay. The CLI mode is separate, and the Native argv keeps its meaning. Identifiers (c+d) still owns the Native cutover.\n\nSecurity: one finding, the required change above. The verifier's level 2 claims that a commit is the cited position of the cited sequence. Bytes that Git reads as a root can pass that check under another genesis. No other finding: option errors throw before I/O, byte sources are bounded at 8 MiB, the Git commands run with a 10-second timeout and a scrubbed environment, and the SHA-1 hash runs in a fresh empty directory with no `-w`.\n\nSimplification: no required change. Two notes for the next head:\n\n- The seal comment for `packages/bytes/src/index.ts` in `security-entry-points.test.mjs` still reads \"Bind the shared address-key export to its reviewed owner\". The file now also exports the citation parsers.\n- A malformed `--source` file, such as one with a duplicate digest, reports `incomplete`/`unavailable` with exit 1, not a usage error with exit 2. `cli.test.ts` pins that answer. The note's \"rejects duplicate digest claims\" can say which answer the reader gives.\n\nThe fix changes `gitEvent`, the producer and the vectors. The cheap checks, the Replay citation tests and a bounded continuation cover it, and the planner rules on the gate. This verdict authorizes no landing, deployment or cutover.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:225c1ccc0d05a3ce27afd31f351474911d33e6c2","binding":"assigned","head":"8793316b4a4affc7e41ada79af521618477878b7","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:90fc277882f5669ebc3074ce99860039bd24ee06\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5a78cc018bfd89dbcd0b6882645b8ca8225455ec\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:90fc277882f5669ebc3074ce99860039bd24ee06","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}